index 1eb4c9095..f719bff68 100644
--- a/docs/investigations/2026-10-tmr-synchronous-entry-dispatch.md
+++ b/docs/investigations/2026-10-tmr-synchronous-entry-dispatch.md
@@ -14,10 +14,19 @@ publishing each reused task slot.
 
 On dispatch, a proven synchronous entry receives `DISPATCH_ORDINARY_ENTRY` in
 the existing local-context flag. The scheduler and executor then skip the cold
-overlap state. A subsequent marked or unknown entry initializes every consumed
-field before publication, so stale state from either physical buffer cannot
-become a predecessor or completion contract. The scheduler also derives a
-marked entry's final prefix and predecessor fields before writing each once.
+overlap state. For a marked or unknown entry, AICPU publishes two immutable
+descriptor words in the preceding cache line. The owning AICore initializes
+every consumed control field on pickup, before inspecting a handoff or calling
+the wrapper. Reusing either physical buffer cannot inherit an old predecessor.
+
+Only that physical AICore writes the mutable control line during an invocation.
+An untraced task therefore does not clean this line at every Wait or FIN.
+Both dispatch-loop EXIT paths clean both buffer control lines before publishing
+EXITED, so retirement cannot release storage with outstanding dirty state.
+The local pipeline drain and full input gate are unchanged. Legacy pending
+flags in the hot header and explicit trace records retain their original
+per-task writeback. An untraced stall dump may observe stale control fields
+until EXIT; this prototype does not promise live control-line diagnostics.
 
 This does not change core selection, readiness, pending dispatch, ACK/FIN
 ordering, `allow_early_resolve`, or the late-arrival notification protocol.
